// Tunable limits, shared by every file in this module.

pub const MAX_HEADERS = 64
pub const MAX_HEADER_BYTES = 65536
pub const MAX_BODY_BYTES = 16777216
// A chunk's extensions (after ';', before the CRLF) are skipped unparsed; this
// bounds how many bytes of them we'll scan before giving up, so a chunk header
// that never reaches its CRLF can't grow the input buffer without limit.
const MAX_CHUNK_EXT_BYTES = 4096
// Once a connection has this much unsent output queued, stop answering further
// pipelined requests from the same read and let it flush first. Without this, a
// client that pipelines many requests and never reads its socket can make the
// server buffer all of their responses in memory at once.
const MAX_PENDING_OUTPUT_BYTES = 1048576
// Server-Sent Events: a blocking send() that doesn't complete within this many
// seconds fails it outright (a hard per-call cap at the socket level, SO_SNDTIMEO).
// On its own this isn't enough: a reader that lets just enough through right
// before each timeout (never actually failing a send, just always slow) could
// string a connection along in increments of this, for as long as it keeps
// timing it right. SSE_SEND_TOTAL_BLOCKED_SECONDS below is what actually bounds
// that, by capping the running total instead of resetting every call.
const SSE_SEND_PER_CALL_TIMEOUT_SECONDS = 5
// Server-Sent Events: once the total time spent so far blocked inside send()
// for one response (ResponseState.blocked, summed across every call, win or
// lose) crosses this, the client is treated as gone — regardless of whether
// the most recent individual send happened to complete. Bounds the worst case
// to roughly this plus one more SSE_SEND_PER_CALL_TIMEOUT_SECONDS, rather than
// an attacker being able to keep narrowly beating each call's own timeout
// forever. Without any of this, a client that opens an events connection and
// never reads it can block that send() forever, freezing this thread's event
// loop (and every other connection on it) for good.
const SSE_SEND_TOTAL_BLOCKED_SECONDS = 30.0
// A connection's input/output buffers start at this size and are reused
// across requests on it. If one ever grows past BUF_SHRINK_THRESHOLD (to
// handle a large request or response), close_conn() shrinks it back down
// rather than letting every connection that was ever used for something
// large permanently keep that peak allocation for as long as the server runs.
const DEFAULT_BUF_BYTES = 16384
const BUF_SHRINK_THRESHOLD = 65536
// The most one sock_recv() call will try to read at once, regardless of how
// much spare capacity a connection's input buffer happens to already have
// from an earlier large request. Keeps one read from pulling in an entire
// multi-megabyte pipelined burst before MAX_PENDING_OUTPUT_BYTES backpressure
// (in answer()/flush_conn()) ever gets a chance to apply.
const MAX_RECV_BYTES = 65536
// How long a connection may sit with no activity (nothing read from it, nothing
// sent to it) before being dropped: covers a connection that opens and sends
// nothing, and an idle keep-alive connection waiting for its next request past
// this point. Checked on the existing once-a-second tick in run_loop, so this
// costs no extra syscalls. Overridable with OXYGEN_IDLE_TIMEOUT_SECONDS (read
// once per worker thread at startup, not on every tick) — mainly so tests
// don't have to wait out the real default.
const DEFAULT_IDLE_TIMEOUT_SECONDS = 60
// How long a connection may sit with its headers still incomplete (bytes
// received, but Conn.header_scan shows no "\r\n\r\n" found yet) before being
// dropped. Shorter than the idle timeout on purpose: a client drip-feeding a
// few header bytes every few seconds (Slowloris) is doing something, just
// slowly and on purpose, so it gets less patience than one simply waiting
// between requests. A request whose headers are complete but whose body is
// still arriving (a slow upload) is not this case — it gets the idle timeout,
// the same as being idle, since that's legitimate. Overridable with
// OXYGEN_HEADER_TIMEOUT_SECONDS, same caveat as above.
const DEFAULT_HEADER_TIMEOUT_SECONDS = 10
